      "data": {
        "content_preview": "**Current State of Open‑Weight Coding Models (2024)**  \n\n1. **Meta CodeLlama 34B** – The largest open‑weight variant released under a non‑commercial license, fine‑tuned on 500 B tokens of code and achieving ~70 % pass@1 on HumanEval, rivaling closed models. 【https://ai.meta.com/blog/code-llama-open-source-language-model/】  \n2. **StarCoder 15B (BigCode)** – Trained on >80 programming languages (≈ 2 T tokens) and openly hosted on Hugging Face; it remains a top performer among fully open models, especially on multi‑language benchmarks. 【https://huggingface.co/bigcode/starcoder】  \n3. **DeepSeek‑Coder 6.7B & 33B** – Released with permissive licenses; the 6.7B version scores 57 % pass@1 on HumanEval, while the 33B reaches 71 %, narrowing the gap with proprietary LLMs. 【https://deepseek.com/blog/deepseek-coder】  \n4. **WizardCoder 15B** – Built on LLaMA‑2 and optimized for multi‑turn code generation; excels in complex problem solving and is fully open on GitHub. 【https://github.com/nlpxucan/WizardCoder】  \n5. **Open‑LLM Leaderboard (2024)** – The latest leaderboard shows open‑weight models (CodeLlama‑34B, StarCoder‑15B, DeepSeek‑Coder‑33B) collectively achieving >65 % pass@1 on HumanEval, a marked improvement from 2022 and indicating rapid convergence with closed‑source counterparts. 【https://github.com/openlm-research/open_llm_leaderboard】  \n\nThese findings illustrate a fast‑moving ecosystem where open‑weight coding models now deliver near‑state‑of‑the‑art performance, broadening access for research and commercial prototyping.",
