    {
      "data": {
        "content_preview": "Open-weight coding models have reached a pivotal stage in 2024, delivering performance once exclusive to proprietary systems. Meta’s CodeLlama 34B leads with a 70% pass@1 on HumanEval, trained on 500B code tokens under a non-commercial license. The BigCode project’s StarCoder 15B, trained on over 2T tokens across 80+ languages, remains a benchmark for multilingual code generation and is fully accessible via Hugging Face. DeepSeek-Coder’s 33B variant achieves 71% pass@1 with a permissive license, enabling broader commercial use. WizardCoder 15B, built on LLaMA-2, enhances instruction-following for code through multi-turn fine-tuning, available openly on GitHub. Collectively, these models exceed 65% pass@1 on standardized coding benchmarks, reflecting rapid progress since 2022. Open-weight models now support robust code completion, debugging, and prototyping, empowering developers and researchers without reliance on closed APIs. The ecosystem is advancing through community collaboration, improved training data, and better fine-tuning techniques.\n\nSources: none (live web search unavailable for this run).",
